# ================= I. INTRODUCTION =================
add('h1', text='I. Introduction')
add('p', text=f"Electronic waste (e-waste) is the fastest-growing solid-waste stream, driven by consumption and short device lifetimes {c(1, 'gem')}. Efficient collection is a prerequisite for recycling, material recovery and safe disposal {c(2)}. E-waste does not arrive evenly across drop-off points and accumulates over time, so a schedule that visits every point at fixed intervals sends vehicles to half-empty points while others overflow. Fixed schedules therefore cost extra distance and stops, under-use vehicle capacity and delay collection from high-demand points; if the collector can estimate how much waste is waiting at each point, part of this cost can be avoided.")
add('p', text=f"Machine-learning forecasts of waste generation are well studied {c(3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9)}, as is waste-collection routing {c(12, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22)}, but the two are usually treated separately. A router that trusts a wrong forecast may overload a vehicle or skip a point about to overflow, yet how forecast error propagates to vehicle loading and service level is rarely examined, and forecasters are typically judged by accuracy rather than by the decisions they induce {c(11, 'hewa')}. This paper addresses three research questions. **RQ1:** Does demand-driven collection outperform fixed schedules when forecasts are accurate, including well-tuned fixed schedules? **RQ2:** Under a capacity constraint, how do service level and cost change as forecast uncertainty grows, and at what uncertainty does the benefit vanish, for realistic (biased, correlated, heavy-tailed) errors and for forecasters actually trained on censored logs? **RQ3:** Can the lost service be recovered with an uncertainty-aware buffer or by tuning the selection threshold, and at what cost?")
add('p', text="The contributions are: (i) a six-layer architecture with a feedback loop that re-estimates forecast uncertainty; (ii) a controlled, fully reproducible simulation that sweeps forecast uncertainty while holding everything else fixed, using common random numbers and held-out test seeds; (iii) break-even uncertainty estimates against both a weak and a tuned fixed baseline, under seven error models; (iv) closed-loop evaluation of trained moving-average, random-forest, XGBoost and LSTM forecasters whose real error σ is measured and placed on the cost-versus-σ curves; (v) tests of estimated-σ̂ pass-through and misestimation, larger instances, alternative depots and fleets, and a stronger solver; (vi) a cost-plus-penalty objective with a Pareto analysis; and (vii) an ablation of the fleet-limit mechanism that explains the capacity anomalies.")

# ================= II. RELATED WORK =================
add('h1', text='II. Related Work and Research Gap')
add('h2', text='A. Waste and e-waste generation forecasting')
add('p', text=f"Regression, tree-ensemble and neural models have been used for municipal waste generation {c(4, 5)} and household e-waste generation {c(3)}; micro-level estimation of e-waste for reverse-logistics planning has been demonstrated for Delhi {c('koshta')}. Gradient-boosted and random-forest ensembles remain strong baselines on tabular data {c(8, 9, 'grins')}, and LSTMs are used for sequences {c(6)}; deep time-series models are surveyed in the forecasting literature. Forecast quality is normally scored by MAPE or scaled errors {c(11)}, and recent guidance warns that such scores can mislead if used without regard to the downstream task {c('hewa')}. Fill-level prediction has also been used directly for bin collection {c('ferrer')}.")
add('h2', text='B. Smart collection, dynamic scheduling and e-waste logistics')
add('p', text=f"Sensor and IoT systems enable fill-level-triggered collection {c(13, 14)} and decision-support reviews summarise them {c(15)}, but they require instrumentation at every point. Dynamic scheduling and routing show that real-time information improves efficiency {c(16)}, and inventory-routing formulations treat collection as reverse replenishment {c(17)}. For e-waste specifically, reverse-logistics reviews {c('islam')}, mixed-fleet collection planning {c('gunawan')} and stochastic e-waste collection solved with deep reinforcement learning {c('nguyen')} have appeared; recent reviews cover sustainable waste-collection routing more broadly {c('li')}. These works treat uncertainty inside the optimiser; they do not isolate how the error of an upstream forecaster translates into overflow under a binding fleet limit.")
add('h2', text='C. Vehicle routing under uncertainty and predict-then-optimise')
add('p', text=f"Waste collection is a capacitated vehicle routing problem (CVRP) {c(18)} surveyed in {c(12, 20, 21)}; constructive savings {c(19)}, modern metaheuristics {c(22, 'filo')} and open-source solvers {c('pyvrp', 'ortools')} give high-quality solutions on standard benchmarks. Demand uncertainty is handled by stochastic routing {c(23, 'oyola', 'gendreau16', 'florio')}, robust optimisation {c(24, 'gounaris')} and distributionally robust chance constraints {c('ghosal')}. A separate line learns decisions directly: machine learning for combinatorial optimisation {c(10)}, “predict, then optimise” {c(25, 'elmach')}, decision-focused learning {c('wilder', 'mandi')} and contextual stochastic optimisation {c('bk', 'sadana')}. Conformal and quantile methods {c('conf')} supply the uncertainty estimates σ̂ that our planner consumes.")
add('table', caption='TABLE I. SUMMARY OF RELATED WORK AND THE GAP ADDRESSED HERE', header=['Theme', 'Refs.', 'Limitation addressed here'], widths=[.30, .22, .48], fs=8, rows=[
 ['Waste / e-waste forecasting', c(3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 'koshta', 'grins'), 'Accuracy reported, not decision impact'],
 ['Smart collection (IoT) and fill-level prediction', c(13, 14, 15, 'ferrer'), 'Needs sensors at every point'],
 ['Dynamic scheduling, inventory routing, e-waste logistics', c(16, 17, 'islam', 'gunawan', 'nguyen', 'li'), 'Assumes reliable state or models uncertainty inside the optimiser'],
 ['CVRP heuristics and solvers', c(12, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 'filo', 'pyvrp', 'ortools'), 'Demand treated as known'],
 ['Stochastic and robust routing', c(23, 24, 'oyola', 'gendreau16', 'florio', 'gounaris', 'ghosal'), 'Error model not tied to a trained forecaster'],
 ['Predict-then-optimise, contextual optimisation', c(10, 25, 'elmach', 'wilder', 'mandi', 'bk', 'sadana'), 'Not studied for capacity-limited e-waste collection']])
add('h2', text='D. Research gap')
add('p', text="Prior work (a) studies prediction and routing in isolation, (b) routes with demand treated as known or drawn from a fixed distribution, (c) mostly considers municipal rather than e-waste streams, and (d) rarely measures the effect of forecast uncertainty on service level and cost when a capacity constraint binds, nor compares against a well-tuned fixed schedule. We address (a), (b) and (d) in an e-waste setting with an integrated architecture and simulation, including closed-loop evaluation of trained forecasters. Point (c) is addressed only through a plausibility check against published generation rates and a loader for real logs; validation on real data remains future work.")

# ================= III. PROBLEM =================
add('h1', text='III. Problem Formulation')
add('p', text="Table II gives the notation. There are N collection points, each with bin capacity B, and a depot. The fill level evolves daily as q_i(t+1) = min(B, q_i(t) + g_i(t)), where the daily generation g_i(t) is stochastic with mean rate λ_i (the cycle-mean λ_i·C is typically well below B but can exceed it for high-rate points over several cycles). Generation exceeding the bin capacity is lost (overflow). Every cycle of C = 3 days, a fleet of K vehicles with capacities Q_1…Q_K may be dispatched; each route starts and ends at the depot, and at each stop the vehicle collects min(q_i, remaining capacity), so an overloaded route leaves some waste behind.")
add('table', caption='TABLE II. NOTATION', header=['Symbol', 'Meaning'], widths=[.25, .75], fs=8.5, rows=[
 ['N, B', 'Number of collection points, bin capacity'], ['q_i(t)', 'True fill level of point i (never observed by the planner)'],
 ['g_i(t), λ_i', 'Daily generation, mean daily rate'], ['K, Q_k', 'Fleet size, capacity of vehicle k'], ['C', 'Planning cycle (days)'],
 ['q̂_i, p̂_i', 'Forecast of current fill; projected fill at the next opportunity'], ['ε_i', 'Multiplicative forecast error factor, q̂_i = q_i·ε_i'],
 ['σ, σ̂', 'True and estimated log-error scale'], ['θ, a, k', 'Base selection threshold, uncertainty shift of the threshold, load-buffer gain'],
 ['c_L', 'Penalty per lost unit in the cost-plus-penalty objective']])
add('p', text="**Information and forecast model.** The planner never observes q_i. At the start of each cycle it holds a forecast q̂_i of the current fill and uses the single error factor ε_i = exp(σ z_i − σ²/2), z_i ~ N(0,1) i.i.d. over points and cycles in the baseline model, so that")
add('eq', text='q̂_i = q_i · ε_i,        p̂_i = q̂_i + C · λ_i,        planned load l_i = min(B, q̂_i · (1 + k σ̂)).')
add('p', text="The same q̂_i is used for both selection (through p̂_i) and loading (through l_i); the earlier draft used two different formulas for these (a multiplicative error on the projected fill in Section III and on the current fill in Section V), which is now unified. λ_i is the point's known mean rate (or its history-based estimate when a trained forecaster is used). The factor ε_i is mean-preserving and lognormal; σ is the only uncertainty parameter and σ ≈ 0.3 corresponds to a typical relative error of about 30%. Section V-C adds persistent-bias, autocorrelated, cross-correlated and heavy-tailed error models.")
add('p', text="**Decision problem.** Each cycle the planner selects a set S of points and builds at most K routes covering S so as to minimise cost per collected unit, (0.9·km + 2.0·stops)/collected units, while keeping overflow loss low. Constraints: (1) every route starts and ends at the depot; (2) each point is visited by at most one route per cycle; (3) the planned load of each route does not exceed the vehicle capacity; (4) at most K routes are dispatched. Because planned loads are estimates, constraint (3) may be violated at execution, producing partial service; and when more than K routes are needed, the extra routes are dropped, which is a second channel through which uncertainty harms service (Section VI-H).")
add('p', text="**Accounting definitions.** Over the measurement window (after warm-up), let S₀ be the total stock at the window start, G the total generation, and S₁ the total stock at the end. Then collected (Col) is the total quantity picked up, lost (Lost) is generation that arrived at a full bin, and residual stock is S₁, the quantity still sitting in bins. The identity")
add('eq', text='S₀ + G = Col + Lost + S₁,        so   Col% + Lost% + Resid% = 100%   with all three expressed as a percentage of (S₀ + G).')
add('p', text=f"holds exactly in every run; the code asserts it, and its maximum absolute violation over all reported runs was {R['identity_max_abs_err']:.1e} units. The earlier draft reported ‘collected’ as a share of generation and ‘lost’ separately, so the two did not sum to 100%; the residual-stock term was missing.")

# ================= IV. ARCHITECTURE =================
add('h1', text='IV. Proposed System Architecture')
add('h2', text='A. Overview')
add('p', text="The framework is a six-layer pipeline (Fig. 1) that converts historical collection records into a per-cycle collection plan. Each layer has a defined input and output, so the forecaster or the routing solver can be swapped without affecting the rest. A feedback path returns logged visits to the data layer, where forecast error is monitored and σ̂ is re-estimated.")
add('img', path=f'{FIG}/fig_arch.png', w=3.3, caption='Fig. 1. Proposed prediction-driven e-waste collection architecture.')
add('h2', text='B. Data sources and preparation (Layers 1–2)')
add('p', text="Layer 1 holds collection logs (point, date, quantity collected), point metadata (type, capacity), calendar information (weekdays, holidays, academic and festival periods), GPS road distances and, optionally, fill-level sensors. Layer 2 cleans the records, aggregates them by point and day, handles missing values and builds features: lagged quantities, rolling means, inter-visit intervals, day-of-week indicators and point attributes. A practical issue is that logs are a censored measurement of generation: a full bin or a full vehicle hides the true amount, so Layer 2 must flag bin-full and vehicle-full visits. Our trained forecasters use exactly these flags.")
add('h2', text='C. Demand forecasting engine (Layer 3)')
add('p', text=f"For each point and cycle the engine estimates the current fill q̂_i (the quantity expected to be present at the visit) and an uncertainty estimate σ̂. We compare a moving-average baseline, random forest {c(7)}, gradient boosting {c(8, 9)} and an LSTM {c(6)}. σ̂ is the rolling root-mean-square of the log ratio between forecast and logged collected quantity over recently visited points; this is what an operator can observe, and Section VI-D shows it understates the true error because logs are censored and selection-biased. σ̂ matters downstream because it sets the conservatism of Layers 4 and 5; quantile or conformal estimates {c('conf')} are natural alternatives.")
add('h2', text='D. Collection-point prioritisation (Layer 4)')
add('p', text="Each point is scored by its projected fill ratio p̂_i/B. Points with p̂_i ≥ θ(σ̂)·B are selected and ranked by descending score, so if the fleet cannot serve everyone, points with the highest overflow risk are served first. When σ̂ is high the system visits points earlier by lowering the threshold: θ(σ̂) = θ − a·min(σ̂, 1), with a = 0.3 in the buffered policy.")
add('h2', text='E. Capacitated route optimiser (Layer 5)')
add('p', text=f"The selected points form a CVRP {c(18)}. Routes are built with the Clarke–Wright savings heuristic {c(19)} and improved by 2-opt, using planned loads l_i = min(B, q̂_i(1 + kσ̂)) as a margin against error. The module can be replaced by a stronger solver such as hybrid genetic search {c(22)} (we test PyVRP {c('pyvrp')} and OR-Tools {c('ortools')} in Section VI-G) or a multi-objective formulation {c(21)}. If more than K routes result, the K routes with the largest planned load are dispatched and the rest dropped; alternatives (re-insertion, priority truncation) are evaluated in Section VI-H.")
add('h2', text='F. Dispatch, execution and feedback (Layer 6)')
add('p', text="Drivers receive route sheets and the quantity collected at each stop is logged. The log feeds Layer 1 so the system can compare forecast and actual quantities, re-estimate σ̂ and retrain the model. When measured error is high the planner becomes more conservative; when it is low it exploits tight, demand-based plans. In the simulation, Layers 3–6 are implemented: forecasting is either a controlled error model (Sections VI-A to VI-C, VI-E) or trained models (Section VI-D).")
add('h2', text='G. Algorithm and complexity')
add('p', text="One planning cycle is given in Algorithm 1. Forecasting is linear in the number of points N for a trained model; the savings heuristic is O(N²) with O(N² log N) sorting; each 2-opt pass is O(n²) in the route size n, negligible for tens of points per cycle.")
add('code', lines=["Algorithm 1: Prediction-driven collection cycle",
"Input: history H, fleet (K, Q), parameters (θ, a, k), uncertainty estimate σ̂",
"1  for each planning cycle do",
"2      forecast current fill q̂_i and projected fill p̂_i = q̂_i + Cλ_i for every point",
"3      S ← {i : p̂_i ≥ (θ − a·min(σ̂,1))·B}, ranked by p̂_i (descending)",
"4      planned load l_i ← min(B, q̂_i(1 + kσ̂))",
"5      routes ← Savings(S, l, Q); improve each route by 2-opt",
"6      dispatch the K routes with the largest planned load (others dropped)",
"7      at each stop collect min(q_i, remaining capacity); log actual loads",
"8      update σ̂ from forecast-versus-logged error; periodically retrain forecaster"])

# ================= V. METHODOLOGY =================
add('h1', text='V. Experimental Methodology')
add('h2', text='A. Policies')
add('p', text=f"**Fixed schedules (baselines).** (i) *Paper baseline*: the alternating-halves schedule (each point visited every 6 days) with a nominal planned load of 0.6B per stop. (ii) *Periodic variants*: every 3 days for all points, every 6 days for all points, 6 days in halves with rate-based planned loads, and every 9 days (all points or thirds). (iii) *Rate-tuned periodic*: each point i is visited every m_i cycles, m_i = clip(⌊ρ·B/(C·λ_i)⌋, 1, 4), with ρ ∈ {{0.6, 0.8, 1.0}} and phases staggered within each period class; this uses the known mean rates, so it is a deliberately informed baseline. The ‘best fixed’ schedule is chosen by cost-plus-penalty on the training seeds; it is **{BF.replace('Fixed-rate-tuned(1.0)', 'rate-tuned with ρ = 1.0')}**.")
add('p', text="**Forecast-driven policies.** *Naive*: θ = 0.85, a = 0, k = 0. *Buffered*: θ = 0.85, a = 0.3, k = 0.8 (the draft's ‘buffered’ policy, i.e. threshold lowered by 0.3·min(σ̂,1) and loads inflated by 1 + 0.8σ̂). *Tuned*: (θ, a, k) chosen per σ by grid search on training seeds. *Known-fill reference*: the naive/tuned policy at σ = 0 (the upper bound for any forecaster). *Reactive MA*: the moving-average forecaster in closed loop (Section V-D).")
add('h2', text='B. Execution, metrics and statistics')
add('p', text=f"Routes for the selected points are built by savings on planned loads and improved by 2-opt; the routes with the K largest planned loads are dispatched, and execution uses actual loads, so forecast error produces partial service. Distances are Euclidean × 1.3. We report distance, stops, collected / lost / residual percentages (summing to 100%), mean fill at visit, and cost per collected unit; for the cost-plus-penalty objective we report J = (0.9·km + 2.0·stops + c_L·lost units)/generated units, with c_L = 0.5 as the tuning objective. Means have 95% t-intervals over instances. Policies are compared on identical instances (common random numbers: generation, initial stock and error draws depend only on the seed) with paired t-tests and, as a non-parametric check, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests {c('wilcox')}. Within each family of comparisons the p-values are Holm-adjusted {c('holm')}; ‘p’ below always means the adjusted value unless stated.")
add('h2', text='C. Error models')
add('p', text="Besides i.i.d. lognormal errors we test, at equal noise scale σ: persistent bias (all forecasts scaled by 0.8 – under-forecasting – or 1.25 – over-forecasting); persistent per-point bias (half the log-variance is a fixed per-point offset); temporal correlation (AR(1) log-error, ρ = 0.8); cross-point correlation (common factor, ρ = 0.5); and heavy tails (Student-t, ν = 3, rescaled to standard deviation σ and clipped at ±3 in log-space, since the unclipped exponential has no finite mean).")
add('h2', text='D. Trained forecasters')
add('p', text="Forecasters are trained on logs generated by the simulator with censoring included: only the quantity actually collected is logged, capped by the bin capacity and by the vehicle's remaining capacity, with bin-full and vehicle-full flags. Features for point i are the days since last visit, the last three collected quantities and inter-visit intervals, implied rates, a cumulative rate, censoring flags, a weekly phase and visit count; the target is log(1 + collected). Models: a moving average (rate × days since last visit), random forest (40 trees), XGBoost (150 trees, depth 5) and a single-layer LSTM (16 units, three-visit sequences, hand-written with back-propagation through time and Adam, because a deep-learning library could not be installed in our environment). Round 0 uses random-visit logs (60 instances; each point visited with probability 0.3–1.0 per cycle); two further rounds add logs from the model's own buffered policy on 40 of these instances (a DAgger-style feedback loop) — training only on random-visit logs produced forecasters that under-predict once they control visits, because skipped points keep growing outside the training range. Training seeds (5000–5059) are disjoint from tuning (0–29) and test seeds (1000–1029).")
add('h2', text='E. Setup, warm-up and initial conditions')
add('table', caption='TABLE III. SIMULATION PARAMETERS', header=['Parameter', 'Value'], widths=[.35, .65], fs=8.5, rows=[
 ['Collection points N', '30, uniform in a 20×20 km square (large instances: area scaled to keep density)'], ['Depot', 'Centre (corner and outside tested)'],
 ['Road detour factor', '1.3 × Euclidean'], ['Mean daily rate λ_i', 'Lognormal, median 8, σ_ln = 0.5'],
 ['Daily generation', 'Gamma (shape 4) × weekly cycle 1 + 0.3 sin(2πd/7)'], ['Bin capacity B', '100 units'],
 ['Vehicles / capacity', '4 / 300 units (default; fleet ratio 1.47)'], ['Planning cycle', '3 days'],
 ['Initial stock', 'q_i(0) ~ U(0, 0.5B), independently per point'], ['Warm-up', '30 days (10 cycles), run under the evaluated policy, excluded from all metrics'],
 ['Measurement horizon', '120 days (40 cycles) after warm-up'], ['Bootstrap for trained forecasters', 'All points visited in the first two cycles to create logs'],
 ['Cost model', '0.9 per km + 2.0 per stop; penalty c_L per lost unit'], ['Seeds', 'Tuning 0–29; test 1000–1029; large instances 2000+; solvers 3000+; forecaster training 5000–5059'],
 ['Tuning grid', 'θ ∈ {0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9}, a ∈ {0, 0.3}, k ∈ {0, 0.4, 0.8}'], ['Replications', '30 test instances (15 / 8 / 4 for N = 50 / 100 / 200; 10 / 4 for solver comparison)']])
add('p', text="The fleet ratio is total fleet capacity divided by the expected demand per cycle (about 816 units for N = 30), so Q = 200, 250, 300, 400 give ratios 0.98, 1.22, 1.47, 1.96. Parameters are assumed rather than calibrated to a real network; Section VI-J gives a plausibility check against published generation rates. Fuel use and emissions are assumed proportional to distance.")

# ================= VI. RESULTS =================
