add('h2', text='B. Effect of forecast uncertainty (RQ2)')
add('p', text=f"Table V and Figure 2 show that service degrades steadily with σ. For the naive policy, overflow loss grows from {fc[('Naive',0)]['lost'].split('±')[0]}% at σ = 0 to {fc[('Naive',0.3)]['lost'].split('±')[0]}% at σ = 0.3, {fc[('Naive',0.5)]['lost'].split('±')[0]}% at σ = 0.5 and {fc[('Naive',1.0)]['lost'].split('±')[0]}% at σ = 1.0. Against the paper baseline ({q['lost_pct']['orig']:.1f}% loss) the service break-even, by linear interpolation of the mean curve, is σ ≈ {bev(be['Naive|loss|orig'])}; against the tuned fixed schedule ({q['lost_pct']['best']:.1f}%) it is σ ≈ {bev(be['Naive|loss|best'])}. In cost-plus-penalty terms (c_L = 0.5) the naive policy stops beating the paper baseline at σ ≈ {bev(be['Naive|J|orig'])} and the tuned baseline at σ ≈ {bev(be['Naive|J|best'])}. The break-even is therefore not a single number: it depends on which fixed schedule is the benchmark. (The draft's single value σ ≈ 0.4 came from a baseline and error model that this revision has replaced; absolute values here differ because the error now enters through the current-fill estimate, Section III.)")
add('p', text="The efficiency metrics need the same caution as before. At high σ the naive policy makes fewer stops and travels less (see Table V) partly because it underestimates accumulation and skips necessary visits, which is why overflow rises; cost per collected unit alone is misleading and must be read together with loss. Partial service due to vehicle overload stayed small.")
