add('p', text=f"Whether the break-even shifts depends on the error structure (Table VI, Fig. 3; breakeven ‘none ≤ 1.0’ means the policy never crosses the benchmark on σ ∈ [0, 1]). For the naive policy the service break-even against the paper baseline moves from σ ≈ {bev(iid_n['be_loss_orig'])} (i.i.d.) to {bev(ee[('Naive','bias_under')]['be_loss_orig'])} with 20% under-forecasting, {bev(ee[('Naive','ar1')]['be_loss_orig'])} with temporally correlated errors, {bev(ee[('Naive','xcorr')]['be_loss_orig'])} with cross-point correlation and {bev(ee[('Naive','bias_point')]['be_loss_orig'])} with persistent per-point bias; over-forecasting ({bev(ee[('Naive','bias_over')]['be_loss_orig'])}) and heavy tails ({bev(ee[('Naive','t')]['be_loss_orig'])}) move it slightly upward. Persistence and correlation hurt because errors do not average out across cycles or points: a point whose fill is under-estimated stays under-estimated, and when all points are under-estimated together the fleet is hit by a correlated shortfall. Against the tuned fixed schedule the break-evens are lower still ({min(bev_b):.2f}–{max(bev_b):.2f}). The buffered policy is more robust: its loss never crosses the paper baseline for i.i.d., under-bias and heavy-tailed errors, but it does cross for AR(1) at σ ≈ {bev(ee[('Buffered','ar1')]['be_loss_orig'])}. Differences from the i.i.d. case at σ = 0.6 are significant for all models except buffered under-bias (Holm-adjusted t and Wilcoxon p < 0.001 in all other cases except the under-bias case, p = {fp(next(r for r in R['err_tests'] if r['policy']=='Buffered' and r['kind']=='bias_under')['pt_holm'])}).")
