add('p', text=f"With i.i.d. errors an online σ̂ (rolling RMS of log forecast/logged ratios over five cycles) is close to the true σ (mean {e1['sig_used']:.2f} vs 0.5 at σ = 0.5; {shr('iid',1.0,'Buffered[est]')['sig_used']:.2f} vs 1.0), and the resulting policy performs about as well as with the true σ (loss {e1['loss']:.2f}% vs {e2['loss']:.2f}%, cost per unit {e1['cpu']:.4f} vs {e2['cpu']:.4f}). Ignoring uncertainty (σ̂ = 0) reverts to the naive policy ({shr('iid',0.5,'Buffered[multx0.0]')['loss']:.2f}% loss at σ = 0.5). Under-estimating σ̂ by half raises loss ({shr('iid',0.5,'Buffered[multx0.5]')['loss']:.2f}%) while over-estimating raises cost ({shr('iid',0.5,'Buffered[multx2.0]')['cpu']:.4f} per unit at 2×σ), and loss is non-monotone in the multiplier: at 1.5–2×σ it rises again ({shr('iid',0.5,'Buffered[multx1.5]')['loss']:.2f}% and {shr('iid',0.5,'Buffered[multx2.0]')['loss']:.2f}%) because inflated planned loads trigger route dropping (Section VI-H). Under persistent under-forecasting the online σ̂ rises to {shr('bias_under',0.5,'Buffered[est]')['sig_used']:.2f} (because the bias enters the RMS), which in this case over-corrects usefully. Misestimation is thus asymmetric and interacts with the fleet limit.")

