# ---- H ablation
add('h2', text='H. Fleet capacity sensitivity and a route-dropping ablation')
ab = R['ablation_rows']
def abv(Q, disp, pol, key='loss'): return next(r for r in ab if r['Q'] == Q and r['dispatch'] == disp and r['policy'] == pol)[key]
rows = []
for Q, ratio in [(200.0, 0.98), (250.0, 1.22), (300.0, 1.47), (400.0, 1.96)]:
    for pol, lab in [(FO, 'Fixed (paper)'), ('Naive', 'Naive'), ('Buffered', 'Buffered')]:
        rows.append([f'{ratio}', lab] + [f"{abv(Q, d, pol):.1f}" for d in ['drop', 'reinsert', 'trunc', 'unlimited']] + [f"{abv(Q, 'drop', pol, 'drop_share'):.0f}%"])
add('table', caption='TABLE XVIII. LOSS (%) AT σ = 0.5 BY FLEET RATIO AND DISPATCH RULE; LAST COLUMN: SHARE OF SELECTED STOPS DROPPED UNDER THE PAPER’S RULE (30 TEST INSTANCES)', header=['Fleet ratio', 'Policy', 'Drop (paper)', 'Re-insert', 'Priority truncation', 'Unlimited fleet', 'Dropped stops'], widths=[.10, .16, .13, .13, .17, .16, .15], fs=7.5, rows=rows)
add('p', text=f"**Mechanism.** The draft attributed the anomalies in capacity sensitivity (the buffered policy being worse than the naive one at ratio 1.22, and the naive policy being worse than the fixed schedule there) to route dropping but did not test it. We now test it by changing only the dispatch rule: *drop* (the K routes with largest planned load are dispatched; the rest dropped), *re-insert* (dropped stops are inserted by cheapest insertion into dispatched routes with spare planned capacity), *priority truncation* (selected points are truncated by projected-fill rank to fit total fleet capacity before routing, then re-insertion) and *unlimited fleet* (all routes are dispatched; a bound, not a feasible policy). At ratio 1.22 the buffered policy drops {abv(250.0,'drop','Buffered','drop_share'):.0f}% of its selected stops under the paper's rule and loses {abv(250.0,'drop','Buffered'):.1f}%; with an unlimited fleet the same selections lose {abv(250.0,'unlimited','Buffered'):.1f}%. The naive policy drops {abv(250.0,'drop','Naive','drop_share'):.0f}% and loses {abv(250.0,'drop','Naive'):.1f}% versus {abv(250.0,'unlimited','Naive'):.1f}% with an unlimited fleet. Route dropping is therefore the dominant cause of the anomaly: the buffer inflates planned loads, fewer points fit per vehicle, more routes are needed than there are vehicles and the lowest-load routes are discarded. Re-insertion and truncation recover only a small part ({abv(250.0,'drop','Buffered')-abv(250.0,'reinsert','Buffered'):.1f} and {abv(250.0,'drop','Buffered')-abv(250.0,'trunc','Buffered'):.1f} pp for the buffered policy at ratio 1.22) because with inflated loads the fleet capacity itself is the binding constraint, not just the routing; the planned capacity is over-booked, which is the mechanism behind the interaction of σ̂-inflation and fleet tightness.")
at = {(r['Q'], r['disp'], r['pol']): r for r in R['ablation_tests']}
an = {(r['Q'], r['disp'], r['pol']): r for r in R['anomaly_rows']}
add('p', text=f"All ablation contrasts were tested with paired t-tests and Wilcoxon tests, Holm-adjusted over the family of 36 contrasts. At ratio 1.22 the unlimited-fleet rule reduces loss by {abs(at[(250.0,'unlimited','Buffered')]['diff']):.1f} pp for the buffered policy (p {fp(at[(250.0,'unlimited','Buffered')]['pt_holm'])}) and {abs(at[(250.0,'unlimited','Naive')]['diff']):.1f} pp for the naive policy (p {fp(at[(250.0,'unlimited','Naive')]['pt_holm'])}); re-insertion changes loss by {at[(250.0,'reinsert','Buffered')]['diff']:+.2f} pp (buffered, p {fp(at[(250.0,'reinsert','Buffered')]['pt_holm'])}) and priority truncation by {at[(250.0,'trunc','Buffered')]['diff']:+.2f} pp (buffered, p {fp(at[(250.0,'trunc','Buffered')]['pt_holm'])}), so the heuristic repairs help little or even hurt. The ‘1.22 anomaly’ itself: under the paper's rule the naive policy loses {an[(250.0,'drop','Naive')]['diff']:+.2f} pp relative to the paper baseline (Holm p {fp(an[(250.0,'drop','Naive')]['pt_holm'])}) and the buffered policy {an[(250.0,'drop','Buffered')]['diff']:+.2f} pp (p {fp(an[(250.0,'drop','Buffered')]['pt_holm'])}); with an unlimited fleet both policies are no longer worse ({an[(250.0,'unlimited','Naive')]['diff']:+.2f} pp, p {fp(an[(250.0,'unlimited','Naive')]['pt_holm'])}; {an[(250.0,'unlimited','Buffered')]['diff']:+.2f} pp, p {fp(an[(250.0,'unlimited','Buffered')]['pt_holm'])}). At ratio 0.98 forecast-driven policies lose far less than the fixed schedule ({abv(200.0,'drop','Naive'):.1f}% / {abv(200.0,'drop','Buffered'):.1f}% vs {abv(200.0,'drop',FO):.1f}%) because prioritisation matters when capacity is scarce, whereas at ratios of 1.47 and above the naive policy's loss is similar to or below the paper baseline ({abv(300.0,'drop','Naive'):.1f}% vs {abv(300.0,'drop',FO):.1f}% at 1.47; {abv(400.0,'drop','Naive'):.1f}% vs {abv(400.0,'drop',FO):.1f}% at 1.96).")
add('img', path=f"{FIG}/fig_ablation.png", w=6.5, caption='Fig. 6. Overflow loss versus fleet ratio under four dispatch rules (σ = 0.5).')
add('p', text="One further observation: at ratio 1.22 the paper-baseline fixed schedule loses less than at 1.47 (5.6% vs 6.1%). Planned loads of 0.6B for 15 stops need three 300-unit routes at Q = 300 (so a vehicle is idle) but four routes at Q = 250, so smaller vehicles are less often overloaded; fleet ratio is thus not monotonically related to loss for a schedule with fixed planned loads.")

# ---- I heterogeneity & J calibration
add('h2', text='I. Sensitivity to demand heterogeneity')
hr = R['het_rows']
add('table', caption='TABLE XIX. SENSITIVITY TO THE DISPERSION OF MEAN RATES σ_ln AT σ = 0.5 (30 TEST INSTANCES; FLEET NOT RESCALED, SO FLEET TIGHTNESS RISES WITH σ_ln; “FIXED” = 6-DAY HALVES, RATE LOADS)', header=['σ_ln', 'Policy', 'Lost %', 'Cost/unit', 'J'], widths=[.10, .35, .18, .22, .15], fs=8,
    rows=[[r['sig_ln'], r['policy'].replace('Fixed-6d-halves', 'Fixed 6d halves').replace('Fixed-rate-tuned(0.8)', 'Fixed rate-tuned (0.8)'), r['loss'], r['cpu'], r['J']] for r in hr])
add('p', text="With nearly homogeneous points (σ_ln = 0.25) schedules lose very little (0.3–0.4%) and forecast-driven policies lose more (4.2%) without a cost advantage over the rate-tuned schedule (J 0.173 vs 0.164); with strongly heterogeneous points (σ_ln = 1.0) every policy loses much more because the fixed fleet becomes tight, and the naive policy's J is about equal to the rate-tuned schedule's. Forecasting thus helps most where rates are very uneven, but this table confounds heterogeneity with fleet tightness.")
add('h2', text='J. Plausibility check against published generation rates')
add('p', text=f"We do not have collection logs from VIT or any authorised recycler; the simulation parameters are therefore assumed, and this subsection is a plausibility check, not a calibration. The Global E-waste Monitor 2024 {c('gem')} reports roughly 4.1 Mt of e-waste generated in India in 2022, i.e. about 2.9 kg per inhabitant per year for a population of about 1.42 billion (derived). If one simulation unit is 1 kg, the median point generates 8 kg/day ≈ 2.9 t/year, which corresponds to a catchment of about 1,000 people if all generated e-waste reached the drop-off point and about 10,000 people at a 10% capture rate. A bin of 100 kg then fills in about 12 days at the median rate. A catchment of this size is consistent with a neighbourhood, campus building cluster or ward-level collection point, and micro-level estimates for Indian cities {c('koshta')} address exactly this scale. The check supports the order of magnitude of λ_i but says nothing about the dispersion (σ_ln), the weekly pattern or the censoring structure of real logs, which is why Table XIX varies σ_ln. The repository includes a script (load_real_logs.py) that estimates censoring-aware point rates and σ_ln from a CSV of real logs, so VIT or recycler data can replace the assumed parameters without changing the simulator.")

# ================= VII..IX =================
add('h1', text='VII. Practical Implications')
add('p', text=f"First, the value of prediction-driven collection depends on both the forecast error and the benchmark: a forecaster should be judged by the collection performance it enables {c(25, 'hewa')}, and against a rate-tuned fixed schedule the achievable gain in this setting is roughly {100*(1-float(fc[('Tuned',0)]['J'])/Jb):.1f}% of cost-plus-penalty even with perfect forecasts. Second, when forecast error is large, uncertainty-aware policies keep service from slipping, consistent with the robustness–cost trade-off {c(24)}, but they are not free (about +9–10% distance at σ = 0.5) and threshold tuning is cheaper than load inflation. Third, the benefit depends on fleet tightness: when capacity is scarce prioritisation helps even with noisy forecasts; when it is ample, a rate-tuned schedule is hard to beat; and load inflation interacts badly with a hard fleet limit. Fourth, before a pilot replaces fixed rounds, the true forecast error should be measured on real data with an audit sample, since σ̂ from censored logs understated σ_eff by a large factor, and the feedback layer should keep tracking it.")
add('h1', text='VIII. Limitations and Threats to Validity')
add('p', text="The study relies on synthetic demand and a simulator whose parameters were chosen by the authors; the only external anchor is a plausibility check against national generation figures, so the absolute values and break-evens are illustrative. Forecaster results are from forecasters trained on simulated logs with a stylised generation process (gamma noise, sinusoidal weekly cycle) and a modest feature set; real logs have richer structure that could make forecasts better or worse. The tuned fixed baseline uses known mean rates while forecasters estimate them. The LSTM is a small hand-written network trained for a fixed number of epochs and not tuned. Routing is heuristic (savings + 2-opt, with PyVRP and OR-Tools checks on a few instances), there is a single depot in most experiments, a homogeneous fleet in the main runs and Euclidean distances with a detour factor. Large-instance and solver comparisons use few replications. Tuning used 30 instances and the selected θ is noisy across σ. The error models share one mean noise scale σ, and the Student-t model is clipped. Multiple-testing correction (Holm) was applied within each family, but the families are our own grouping. All conclusions should be re-checked on real collection logs before operational use.")
add('h1', text='IX. Conclusion and Future Work')
sgn = lambda x: f"{x:.2f}"
add('p', text=f"We proposed a six-layer prediction-driven architecture for e-waste collection and measured, by reproducible simulation with held-out seeds, how forecast uncertainty affects it under capacity constraints. With perfect forecasts demand-driven collection reduces stops by {abs(red('stops')):.1f}%, distance by {abs(red('dist')):.1f}% and cost per unit by {abs(red('cpu')):.1f}% relative to the alternating-halves schedule, but only {abs(redb('cpu')):.1f}% on cost relative to a rate-tuned fixed schedule, with its main benefit being service (loss {q['lost_pct']['fc']:.1f}% vs {q['lost_pct']['best']:.1f}%). The service break-even is σ ≈ {sgn(be['Naive|loss|orig'])} for the naive policy against the original schedule and σ ≈ {sgn(be['Naive|loss|best'])} against the tuned one; correlated or under-forecast errors lower the former to {sgn(min(bev_o))}–{sgn(errs[('Naive','xcorr')]['be_loss_orig'])}, while over-forecasting and heavy tails raise it. The uncertainty buffer reduces loss by {abs(b05['lost_pct']['diff']):.1f} pp at σ = 0.5 at the price of {b05['dist']['pct']:.0f}% more distance and {b05['cpu']['pct']:.0f}% more cost per unit versus naive; its failures at tight fleet ratios are due to route dropping, confirmed by ablation. Trained forecasters have measured σ of {sgn(min(sef))}–{sgn(max(sef))}, beat the paper baseline but not the tuned schedule on cost-plus-penalty, and online σ̂ from logs underestimates their error. Future work: validation on real e-waste logs (VIT or authorised recyclers), forecasters trained on real data with conformal σ̂, time windows, emissions objectives, stochastic-routing and decision-focused-learning baselines {c('florio', 'mandi', 'sadana')}, and joint optimisation of the visit schedule and forecaster.")
